/**
 * Approach:
 * Use a Min Heap (Priority Queue) to always pick the smallest node among the current heads of all linked lists.
 * Initially, insert only the head node of every linked list into the heap.
 * Remove the smallest node, attach it to the answer, and insert its next node into the heap. Repeat until the heap becomes empty.

 **/

class Solution {
    public ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {   //ListNode[] lists -It is an array that stores only the head of each linked list.

        // Min Heap based on node value
        PriorityQueue<ListNode> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> a.val - b.val
        );

        // Insert the head node of every linked list
        for (int i = 0; i < lists.length; i++) {
            if (lists[i] != null) {
                pq.offer(lists[i]);
            }
        }

        // Dummy node for building the merged list
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(-1);
        ListNode temp = dummy;

        // Continue until all nodes are processed
        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {

            // Get the smallest node   //This is because PriorityQueue is implemented using a Min Heap.you are telling Java:"Always keep the node with the smallest val at the top."
            ListNode node = pq.poll();

            // Attach it to the merged list
            temp.next = node;
            temp = temp.next;

            // Insert the next node from the same linked list
            if (node.next != null) {
                pq.offer(node.next);
            }
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
}
/*
Approach:
1. Use a Min Heap (Priority Queue) to always get the smallest node.
2. Initially, insert only the head node of each linked list into the heap.
3. Remove the smallest node and attach it to the answer.
4. If the removed node has a next node, insert it into the heap.
5. Repeat until the heap becomes empty.

Time Complexity: O(N log K)
- N = Total number of nodes.
- K = Number of linked lists.
- Each node is inserted into the heap once and removed once.
- Every offer() and poll() operation takes O(log K).
- Therefore, total time = O(N log K).

Space Complexity: O(K)
- The Priority Queue stores at most one node from each linked list.
- So the maximum size of the heap is K.
*/
